<http://data.silknow.org/object/0b3ddba3-9113-344c-90b3-871cbdd36600/observation/1>
        a                   crmsci:S4_Observation ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    <http://data.silknow.org/observation/technical-observation> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "Woven scarf with a plain red silk centre and narrow applied borders of woven silk and wool. The borders at each end are 5.5 inches deep, and are filled with geometric shapes containing the small flowers derived from the 'cones' in Indian and Persian shawls. In the main part of the border are inverted triangles, themselves containing other triangular shapes filled with flower-heads and flower-sprays, that hang from the top edge. Their points touch the highest parts of the semicircles, also flower-filled, which rise from the lower edge. Between the semicircles are small triangles from the tips of which spring delicate trees bearing flowers, similar to the 'cones', but in this case symmetrically shaped. Along the lower edge of this patterned band is a narrow strip containing reciprocal triangles filled with flower sprays and tiny flowering trees, and the whole is flanked by very narrow guard stripes.\n\nAlong the two sides of the scarf is a border about 0.5 inch wide containing a floral meander.\n\nBorders are woven in red, pink, green, pale blue, lilac, mustard yellow, orange and black wool, and white silk in 1/3 twill on a red silk warp."@en ;
        crmsci:O8_observed  <http://data.silknow.org/object/0b3ddba3-9113-344c-90b3-871cbdd36600> .

